  The record's PN-4, reproduced: Yb's sibling term is 0.1098 of 0.1762 = 62 % (record: 0.112 of 0.179, 63 %),
  against the 4f class residual of +0.09..+0.1 Ha the term-resolved object leaves.

  PROTACTINIUM, AND WHETHER THE 4f CLASS RESIDUAL TRANSFERS TO IT.  It does not, on either of the
  record's own readings of that residual:

    by SIBLING COUNT -- the mechanism PN-4 names.  Ytterbium's 4f14 -> 4f13 loses 13 sibling pairs,
    0.10981 Ha, or 0.00845 Ha each.  Protactinium's 5f2 -> 5f1 loses ONE:
    0.00338 Ha = 0.092 eV on its own orbital, 0.230 eV if a 5f pair
    correlated like an Yb 4f pair.  Against the class residual of +0.09..+0.10 Ha (2.45..2.72 eV), that is smaller by a factor of twenty to thirty.

    by COMPACTNESS -- the variable of the Janak class law.  <r> is 0.760 a0 for Yb's 4f
    and 1.421 a0 for Pa's 5f: protactinium's entrant is nearly twice as diffuse, and sits
    with the 5d rows the record measured at -0.008..-0.012 Ha, not with the 4f rows.

    the core part, which does not depend on how many siblings there are: -0.05104 Ha,
    so E2_ent = -0.05442 Ha under the general scaling.

  THE LMAX CONVERGENCE BRIDGE 34 ORDERED AND NEVER RAN  ("run LMAX=4/5 convergence ... before reading")

   LMAX |  Pa E2closed   Yb E2closed |  Pa 3H/pair  Yb 3H/pair  per-pair Pa/Yb |  Pa's ONE pair  Yb's 13   ratio
    3   |    -0.30782     -0.76866 |   -0.002178   -0.005591            0.400 |      0.002178   0.10981    50.4
    4   |    -0.76551     -1.11060 |   -0.004699   -0.007440            0.689 |      0.004699   0.15866    33.8
    5   |    -0.83041     -1.16650 |   -0.004944   -0.007675            0.712 |      0.004944   0.16664    33.7

   The ABSOLUTE sums are badly unconverged at the record's LMAX = 3: ytterbium's sibling term runs
   0.110 -> 0.159 -> 0.167 and protactinium's closed-shell sum 0.308 -> 0.766 -> 0.830.  The record
   banked LMAX = 3 and read PN-4 off it as "0.09 vs 0.11"; at convergence it is 0.09 against 0.167,
   so the frozen second-order estimate overshoots the residual it was tested against by 85 %, not by
   22 %.  The record's own two cautions -- frozen second order overestimates (He x1.3) and the ion's
   relaxation is absent -- must therefore carry about HALF the value, not a fifth of it.

   The PER-PAIR ratio moves the other way and corrects this instrument's first reading: Pa/Yb runs
   0.400 -> 0.689 -> 0.712, so at convergence protactinium's 5f pair correlates within 30 % of an
   ytterbium 4f pair, NOT at two-fifths of it.  The compactness half of FINDING-R4-16 s4 was read off
   an unconverged number and is corrected there.  What survives, and it is exact arithmetic rather
   than a computed quantity, is the SIBLING COUNT: protactinium loses ONE pair where ytterbium loses
   THIRTEEN, and the ratio of the two totals is 33.7 at convergence.

  THE TERM DECOMPOSITION, AND WHY PROTACTINIUM'S OWN PAIR IS THE WEAKEST ONE  (Pa, LMAX = 5)

    term  weight   contribution   per pair    per pair / average
    1S       1       -0.04559   -0.045589              4.996
    3P       9       -0.15232   -0.016925              1.855
    1D       5       -0.07928   -0.015855              1.738
    3F      21       -0.17102   -0.008144              0.892
    1G       9       -0.08332   -0.009258              1.015
    3H      33       -0.16315   -0.004944              0.542   <-- Pa I's 4K11/2 is 5f2(3H) + 6d
    1I      13       -0.13573   -0.010441              1.144

    The seven allowed terms of f^2 carry weights summing to 91, which IS the pair count of a closed
    f14 shell, so the closed-shell sum is literally a sum over terms.  Protactinium's two 5f electrons
    sit in 3H -- maximum multiplicity and maximum L, where Hund's rules hold them furthest apart -- and
    it is the weakest-correlating term of the seven, at 0.54 of the average, against 5.0 for the 1S singlet.
    So the average pair is the wrong quantity for protactinium and the term-resolved one is right.

  WHAT THIS IS AND IS NOT.  The record's own reading governs: frozen second order on local orbitals
  OVERESTIMATES (He x1.3), and the ion's own correlation relaxation, which reduces the removal
  correlation, is ABSENT here.  So this is a lower-bound-shaped estimate of what the removal energy is
  missing, not a correction to add.  The record tested it at Yb -- 0.11 against a 0.09 residual -- and
  left the object open through bridge 34.  Nothing is closed here and nothing enters a volume.

